From c558623439990179f28a04c9cc300348991de8f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MaiRat <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 06:05:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Route v-mode class sets and property escapes; unify and
 null-tolerate the match path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three track-2 changes on the Broiler.JS side of the RegExp integration.

Routing (action 5): the gap scan routes a v-mode class-set expression — `[a&&b]`,
`[a--b]`, `[[a][b]]`, `\q{…}` — and a property escape used as a class member. Both are
shapes the source-to-source translator can only approximate: it has to evaluate the set
itself, and inside `[…]` it falls back to .NET's code-unit-based `\p{Lu}`. Broiler.Regex
evaluates both directly. `UsesUnsupported` — which existed to refuse a class-set pattern
the matcher would have mis-matched — has nothing left to refuse and is gone.

Match data (action 4): `exec` already read either engine through `RunMatch`; now `Split`
and `Replace` do too, via a shared `EnumerateMatches` iterator over `RunMatch`
(code-point-aware empty-match advance, `lastIndex` left untouched exactly as the .NET path
left it). So the legacy `String.prototype.split`/`replace` fallback — reached when a
RegExp's `@@split`/`@@replace` is removed — answers a routed gap pattern with Broiler's
match data instead of the .NET translation's (`(a?b??)*` on "ab" matches "ab", not "a").
`IJSRegExp.Value` is retired for an engine-agnostic `IsMatch`, so `assert.match` routes too.

Null-tolerant translation (the tail of action 4): now that no supported operation reads
the .NET engine for a routed pattern, `CreateRegex` wraps the translation so that — for a
routed pattern only — a transform's "not supported" error or a `new Regex` rejection falls
back to a null `value`, and the RegExp runs on Broiler alone. A v-mode set operation over a
built-in class escape (`[\s&&\S]`, `[\d&&\s]`, `[\s--\d]`) is the concrete case: valid
ECMAScript the .NET UnicodeSets translator rejects, which now constructs and matches
(exec/test/split/replace) in agreement with V8. A pattern invalid in both engines is not
routed — Broiler rejects it, so the fallback is skipped — and still throws a SyntaxError.

RegExpEngineConsistencyTests and RegExpTranslatorFallbackTests cover both, plus a batch of
v-mode set operations compared to V8. BuiltIns.Tests 2191 (up from 2173); the Integration
suite's one failure, M8_DocumentationFiles_Exist, is pre-existing and unrelated.

Requires the matching Broiler.Regex revision (its CharSet.UsesPropertyEscape and
MatchesEmptyString).
